{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure node1 as a persistent RADIUS server for node2, using a dedicated protected client secret and the demonstration user/password from access.txt.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node2 client is restricted to 10.100.163.11, its secret matches protected files on both nodes, and the configured user matches access.txt. After node1 restarted, radiusd, its listener, firewall rule, client block, and user block recovered; authentication from node2 still worked.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4116361b31af473d","cmd-7f405b3eae9b418d","cmd-1f744f9c9fbf41fb","cmd-4f11dd02f0434a79","cmd-d90ad5aaf2ee47b6"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Correct demonstration credentials receive Access-Accept.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final post-restart matrix directly records one approved authentication, response code 2, with no denied or lost requests.","evidence_ids":["cmd-757d6630c96a42b0"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Wrong credentials receive Access-Reject.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final post-restart matrix directly records response code 3 and one denied authentication with no approvals or losses.","evidence_ids":["cmd-757d6630c96a42b0"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Requests using the wrong client secret are not accepted.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final matrix shows zero approvals and an invalid-signature/shared-secret error; the request received no valid accepted response.","evidence_ids":["cmd-757d6630c96a42b0"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the handover in /srv/legacy2/radius-network-authentication intact.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both handover files have identical checksums before configuration and after configuration plus both restarts; the initial prepared inventory also shows these were the handover's two regular files.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-6aadd49122cd4d75","cmd-4f11dd02f0434a79","cmd-7f405b3eae9b418d","cmd-9f55bd667d114dbe"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on CentOS 5.11 with kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 after restart. Signed CentOS 5 FreeRADIUS 2.1.12 packages were installed and demonstrated working.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7ab2174a2b2a4371","cmd-4850c4ccf4024783","cmd-b7973e3312574585","cmd-1f744f9c9fbf41fb","cmd-9f55bd667d114dbe","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.77,"summary":"Functionality and security scoping are good, but cleanup was incomplete. Retained executor residue includes downloaded RPMs, index files, and reboot logs under /tmp on both nodes; these were created by package discovery/install and restart commands and remained in the after-executor inventories (cmd-2d1894899d6e402c, cmd-38466efe1bfd41c7, cmd-62a4171ab8aa43b5, cmd-4850c4ccf4024783, cmd-b7973e3312574585, cmd-a75fa3a3f06f4e15, cmd-09b65e9dd2f149f8). Configuration backup files clients.conf.pre-legacy2 and users.pre-legacy2 were created and no cleanup is shown in the complete trace (cmd-7867303f8fbf4e58). A failed Ed25519 transfer path wrote a private key and known-host data but did not contribute to the eventual RSA-based transfer; that low-risk experiment was later reverted (cmd-4c25a9d353fc41fb, cmd-49bde385a1514888). Required temporary secrets and generated RSA transfer keys were removed, and the firewall exposure is limited to node2.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2d1894899d6e402c","cmd-38466efe1bfd41c7","cmd-62a4171ab8aa43b5","cmd-4850c4ccf4024783","cmd-b7973e3312574585","cmd-7867303f8fbf4e58","cmd-4c25a9d353fc41fb","cmd-49bde385a1514888","cmd-a75fa3a3f06f4e15","cmd-09b65e9dd2f149f8","cmd-ddb1536e6fb14d0e","cmd-7007e11f1cac4097","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct post-restart behavior. Correct and incorrect credentials produce the required RADIUS responses, a wrong client secret is not accepted, the handover remains unchanged, and the legacy OS/kernel are retained. The mandatory restart protocol is strong for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, recovered data/configuration, and successful authentication afterward. The principal deficiency is operational residue in /tmp and unremoved configuration backups.","limitations":["The lifecycle listening-socket collector returned an empty inventory even though direct final commands showed radiusd listening and successful network authentication; the behavioral evidence is stronger, but the collector discrepancy slightly lowers confidence.","Systemd failed-service checks were unsupported on this legacy platform, and the lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation, so those supplemental checks cannot establish general service or package health.","cmd-9e293748923b45e2 conditionally added a cluster SSH public key to node1. The trace proves the key was present afterward but cannot establish whether it was newly appended or pre-existing; cleanup only explicitly targeted the later generated RSA key."]}
